  "description": "Strings primer: immutability and builders, int[26] counting, two pointers and read/write heads in place, palindromes by expanding around 2n-1 centres, parsing with a tiny state machine (atoi, valid number, version compare), length-prefix encode/decode, and an intro to KMP and rolling hash, with Go, C++, Java and Python templates.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\r\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\r\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"2\">Level 2 - Beginner</span><span class=\"time\">40 min</span>\r\n    <span class=\"chip accent\">Pattern primer</span><span class=\"chip\">Strings</span>\r\n  </div>\r\n  <h1>Strings</h1>\r\n  <p class=\"lead\">A string is an array of characters with three extra traps: it may be immutable, it may hide its length, and its input is messy. Learn the four moves that solve most interview string problems: two pointers in place, expand around the centre, parse with a tiny state machine, and length-prefix encoding. Then a first look at pattern matching (KMP) so a follow-up never surprises you.</p>\r\n  <p class=\"small prereq\"><b>Go deeper:</b> <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-string-matching\">String matching: KMP, Z-function, rolling hash</a>, <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-palindrome-interval-dp\">Palindrome DP and interval DP</a>.</p>\r\n"
  },
